1
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:27,430
Software Engineering Radio episode 6 Model Driven Software Development part two.

2
00:00:29,230 --> 00:00:33,270
This is the second part of our introduction to Model Driven Software Development.

3
00:00:33,270 --> 00:00:43,030
In the first episode on this topic we talked about what Model Driven Development is all about about the core concepts about the tooling about some of the MDA standard.

4
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:50,550
In this episode we'll talk more about the soft topics like how do you introduce MDSD into projects?

5
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:55,230
How do you make sure all your all your developers can understand the technology?

6
00:00:55,230 --> 00:00:58,870
How do you combine MDSD with Agile Development?

7
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:05,070
Have fun. So with all these metamodels and metametamodels and

8
00:01:05,070 --> 00:01:16,710
models and whatever I'm wondering whether you what this means for the kind of programmer that you need to have in your projects.

9
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:18,710
So is it possible to do this on your own?

10
00:01:18,710 --> 00:01:30,110
Do you need to have some kind of super programmers that know everything about EMF and UML and the UML tool and metamodels and metamodel transformations and what have you?

11
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:37,310
Well, of course using Model Driven Development is is a shift from traditional development.

12
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:41,230
Not just because you have to know and understand all those passwords.

13
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:49,950
Also because you know, not all projects think well and extensively about their architecture.

14
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:52,070
So being forced to make that explicit is also a problem.

15
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:57,430
Of course you have to learn all those tools and some of the concepts.

16
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:08,950
And of course if you start a project it's probably not good to start a new project in a new domain with a new technology and a new platform and then also introducing MDSD because that would be too much.

17
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:22,710
So when starting to use MDSD you should do this in a familiar environment with your you know, you've used J2EE for five projects and you use it again and now you use the automation provided by MDSD.

18
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:24,350
So this is the first important thing.

19
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:29,590
Second thing is that there are two different roles in MDSD based projects.

20
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:37,070
The first role is actually those folks who you know, formalize the architecture and build the generator define the metamodels.

21
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:43,830
This is what we call the domain architecture development track or threat or path.

22
00:02:43,830 --> 00:02:52,190
And then there is the other group of people who use who use this um generator to yeah, build applications.

23
00:02:52,190 --> 00:02:56,070
And those people don't have to understand all those fancy things.

24
00:02:56,070 --> 00:03:04,230
They just have to be able to draw or write up models and then start generator and know how to work with what the generator spits out.

25
00:03:04,230 --> 00:03:07,590
And of course that might be non-trivial.

26
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:14,230
So you have to document the programming model and how to use the generator, how to use the DSL.

27
00:03:14,230 --> 00:03:20,230
But this rather magic stuff you know, one or two meta levels higher that's something only a limited group of people should usually use.

28
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:29,950
Also my experience is that being able to formalize things into metamodels and working on that conceptual level is not everybody's not everybody can easily do that.

29
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:34,350
It's it's to some extent you you can learn it but not completely.

30
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:39,830
There has to be you know, some kind of predisposition for people being able to do that.

31
00:03:39,830 --> 00:03:43,630
So there are some folks in projects who usually like doing that.

32
00:03:43,630 --> 00:03:51,750
I mean of course you have to make sure that they you know, don't start you know, drifting off and and coming up with rather cool generators that don't solve anybody's problem.

33
00:03:51,750 --> 00:03:54,870
But you have to make sure that you have those two different groups of people.

34
00:03:54,870 --> 00:04:04,550
For the people who who like doing that and who kind of want to do that in projects these people understand it reasonably well fast.

35
00:04:04,550 --> 00:04:10,350
On the other hand I think and that's not I'm not saying that because I I want to sell consulting hours or anything.

36
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:22,990
It's my experience is really that it helps at the beginning of a project to have somebody that has done it before giving you some advice avoiding the basic pitfalls and maybe also showing you how to work with the tool you want to use.

37
00:04:22,990 --> 00:04:28,030
So that because it is new in several respects it it does make sense to have somebody help you.

38
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:42,150
So that's basically something that that you can say for everything that you have to introduce new into an organization that you have to have somebody that is experienced and external help can help and so on and so on.

39
00:04:42,150 --> 00:04:45,630
So that's probably not so so surprising.

40
00:04:45,630 --> 00:04:53,630
The other thing that I'm wondering is at the end of the day we are talking about automation and usually this means that

41
00:04:53,630 --> 00:05:00,710
you generate a payoff after doing the same thing several times.

42
00:05:00,710 --> 00:05:05,870
So usually you would expect that you would have to do a significant investment into automation and

43
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:13,150
you get a payoff after some projects that benefit from the automation to do less manual work.

44
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:25,790
So what I'm wondering is how many projects do I have to have of the same kind until my generator and my investment into automation pays off?

45
00:05:25,790 --> 00:05:28,190
Can you give any hints about this from your experience?

46
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:33,030
So of course in in in the case of MDSD the economies of scale hit you hard.

47
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:42,750
That means if you build an infrastructure you know, metamodels generators and stuff that you can use over and over and over and over again then this is obviously an advantage.

48
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:47,030
So if you work in software system families where product lines where

49
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:59,270
you build all kinds of applications based on the same domain architecture then this is an advantage obviously and it's probably easy to argue that in such a setting it's good to use the approach we just talked about.

50
00:05:59,270 --> 00:06:11,590
So the challenge is how do I make sure that using MDSD already pays off much earlier and not only you know, after in three years because almost nobody invests into something that pays off after three years.

51
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:19,990
So what I always say and the funny thing is that all the people I know who have really experience in MDSD they will say exactly the same.

52
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,830
If introducing MDSD doesn't pay off in the first project you did something wrong.

53
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:30,230
So you have to do it pragmatically of course.

54
00:06:30,230 --> 00:06:35,750
You can't you shouldn't try to aim at the holy grail of real domain specific stuff as we talked about before.

55
00:06:35,750 --> 00:06:46,270
But if you automate your architecture um sensibly then this can save you so much work that this pays off after a couple of weeks or months.

56
00:06:46,270 --> 00:06:57,150
Of course there is one precondition if you count in learning all the tools into one project then that might not be true.

57
00:06:57,150 --> 00:07:07,230
But if you have some external help for a week or two and they set you up the basic infrastructure and teach you some best practices and some anti-patterns then I would say that in a project you know,

58
00:07:07,230 --> 00:07:11,510
like seven or eight people working for a couple of months using MDSD will pay off.

59
00:07:11,510 --> 00:07:20,630
This is definitely my experience and it's not just about you know, spending less work coming you know, spitting out the code.

60
00:07:20,630 --> 00:07:25,150
It's also your your your software quality improves because generators don't make accidental errors.

61
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:34,350
They're either always right or always wrong and things that are always wrong can more easily be debugged than things that are only are only occasionally wrong.

62
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:36,430
And also you can more easily change the platform.

63
00:07:36,430 --> 00:07:46,990
If you want to migrate for example from Hibernate to TopLink then this is rather easy because if you didn't do things completely wrong then you just have to adapt your code generation templates.

64
00:07:46,990 --> 00:07:52,870
So what I'm saying is that in addition to spending less effort and doing you know,

65
00:07:52,870 --> 00:08:03,910
having less work to do you also improve maintainability and software quality and and also you you kind of capture best practices about about your architecture in in in in templates and metamodels.

66
00:08:03,910 --> 00:08:13,630
So there are all kinds of kind of non-countable benefits that are really important and so if if you take that into account it it pays off really early.

67
00:08:13,630 --> 00:08:25,750
So the the thing to note here is it's not just about automation it's also about architecture centric architecture centric view and how this improves software development in general.

68
00:08:25,760 --> 00:08:36,149
So and and also the the effort at least from what I've seen to

69
00:08:36,149 --> 00:08:41,350
implement such a generator is not as big as you would probably think when when you hear about that.

70
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:44,190
Right.

71
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:52,750
So to today's tools are I wouldn't say sophisticated because compared to you know, mainstream IDEs like Eclipse it's still quite in its infancy.

72
00:08:52,750 --> 00:09:02,030
But those tools are really not too bad and you can work productively with them and so getting into those tools is not as bad as it might look if you look from the outside.

73
00:09:02,030 --> 00:09:13,950
So is this the weapon that we've been looking for to fight offshoring or is it not enough to get this payoff to keep programming jobs in Europe or the US?

74
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:15,510
Yeah, this is an interesting topic.

75
00:09:15,510 --> 00:09:19,270
I mean why will people offshore things?

76
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:24,230
One thing is because of political reasons.

77
00:09:24,230 --> 00:09:32,590
Some you know, boss CEO or C whatever O decided that strategically 50% of all development effort has to be outsourced or offshore to some wherever.

78
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:34,470
So I can't argue against that.

79
00:09:34,470 --> 00:09:38,910
That's political strategic I mean not my problem.

80
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:44,590
What I can say is that if you want if you think about offshoring because you have a real reason for it which

81
00:09:44,590 --> 00:09:51,670
means that you can have a lot of work being done for less money then using MDSD can be an alternative and and that is in two ways.

82
00:09:51,670 --> 00:10:03,750
First of all, if you automate then the brain dead work that you are probably inclined to out offshore is it doesn't happen anymore because the generator does that for you.

83
00:10:03,750 --> 00:10:12,470
So, you know, you don't have those large amounts of stupid things that people have to do over and over and over and over again and that's too expensive and therefore you have to offshore it.

84
00:10:12,470 --> 00:10:17,630
So, that's that's one argument which is against offshoring and pro MDSD, of course.

85
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:28,350
The other thing is that if you have to offshore, then the challenge is to off to the offshore in a way that the core know-how about your architecture and your stays in-house.

86
00:10:28,350 --> 00:10:29,870
You don't want to offshore your knowledge.

87
00:10:29,870 --> 00:10:31,590
You want to offshore work.

88
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:37,230
And if you do that, you can use MDSD to ensure that.

89
00:10:37,230 --> 00:10:46,230
So, you can you can for example define the architecture and the generators locally and define a programming model that is well restricted by using generators and

90
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:50,470
all kinds of things, patterns, and stuff.

91
00:10:50,470 --> 00:10:56,230
And then you can let people do the the implementation of the business logic uh somewhere else.

92
00:10:56,230 --> 00:11:04,030
So, you could uh for example come up with uh some specifications using your DSLs locally and

93
00:11:04,030 --> 00:11:15,110
then ship that over to uh the offshore location where people then add some of the business logic and maybe draw the GUIs which are often hard to generate if you want it um to look really nice.

94
00:11:15,110 --> 00:11:20,950
Um so, there are other means by which MDSD can help make offshoring more feasible.

95
00:11:20,950 --> 00:11:25,110
However, to be honest, I think if you do MDSD right, you don't want to offshore.

96
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:31,390
And and do you think it's important that um that

97
00:11:31,390 --> 00:11:39,510
you reach the holy grail of doing MDSD uh with real domain specific language to uh

98
00:11:39,510 --> 00:11:45,990
to to uh be able to compete against offshoring or is it already enough to have this architecture centric uh MDSD approach?

99
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,270
It really depends um what you want to achieve with offshoring.

100
00:11:51,270 --> 00:12:00,950
If it's really that you want to have those huge amounts of work being done cheaper, then you can automate large part of that and you wouldn't want to uh offshore anymore.

101
00:12:00,950 --> 00:12:06,310
Um So, actually I'm to be honest, I don't really I don't know.

102
00:12:06,310 --> 00:12:18,511
I mean, I if I had to decide between offshoring and model driven, uh obviously I would use model driven and that's also because you know those risks of communication and different cultures and time zones and all that stuff. That's not there.

103
00:12:18,511 --> 00:12:24,390
Of course, you have risks too if you go to this new MDSD technology, but it's it's something that's a bit more controlled.

104
00:12:24,390 --> 00:12:29,950
Now, of course, on the other side I had I have to say that I have more experience with MDSD than with offshoring.

105
00:12:29,950 --> 00:12:32,910
So, maybe I'm not quite quite fair here,

106
00:12:32,910 --> 00:12:40,790
but to me it seems that automating things is better than getting huge amounts of people to do cheap work because,

107
00:12:40,790 --> 00:12:45,790
you know, they all have to be coordinated and managed and all these things.

108
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:48,190
So, I'm I'm I'm really skeptical about the offshoring thing.

109
00:12:48,200 --> 00:13:02,790
An interesting idea to have MDSD to solve part of the communication problem that you already or those have in software development projects because there are these people that have to be coordinated and so on.

110
00:13:02,790 --> 00:13:04,950
So, one other thing that I'm wondering is I mean,

111
00:13:04,950 --> 00:13:12,070
the role of the model is um is pretty much a formalized model and it's uh becoming a software artifact and

112
00:13:12,080 --> 00:13:19,070
that's quite different from what for example agility says because agility says,

113
00:13:19,070 --> 00:13:25,910
"Okay, so models are one part of communication and it's something that I draw somewhere on a whiteboard or whatever and if everybody says,

114
00:13:25,910 --> 00:13:33,470
"Okay, this is what we agree upon and we all know what this means and it aids us in communication."

115
00:13:33,470 --> 00:13:45,631
That's what we want from the models and that's pretty much different from what MDSD says because it says, "Okay, this has to be formalized and it's one of part of the code that we are going to produce." And so on.

116
00:13:45,631 --> 00:13:54,190
So, do you think that the you can actually use MDSD in an Agile project?

117
00:13:54,190 --> 00:13:56,270
I have never used MDSD in any other context than in Agile project.

118
00:13:56,280 --> 00:14:03,950
So, I'm not sure if this this discussion between Agile and model driven is still so important today.

119
00:14:03,950 --> 00:14:12,670
Two or three years ago it was important because it was kind of, you know, "Oh, the case tool folks come back and now they're calling it MDSD and we don't want that."

120
00:14:12,670 --> 00:14:16,630
I think those two approaches don't contradict each other at all.

121
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:22,510
Um I'm going as far as to say that you can't be Agile without being model driven.

122
00:14:22,510 --> 00:14:31,350
Now, that's probably too far, but um what I'm trying to say is that if you do MDSD right, then it helps you to become more Agile.

123
00:14:31,350 --> 00:14:38,350
Examples, um if you want to migrate your project, your code from one technical infrastructure to another,

124
00:14:38,350 --> 00:14:48,430
for example, to a new version of J2EE or something, then using model driven development, this is much easier because you only have to change things in one place, templates, code generation templates.

125
00:14:48,440 --> 00:14:56,190
So, this makes you more Agile because you can respond to change in technical aspects more quickly.

126
00:14:56,190 --> 00:14:59,910
The same is true to uh for functional non-technical things.

127
00:14:59,910 --> 00:15:08,190
Like if your customer has different algorithms, different different you know, attributes for your entities, different uh GUI layout,

128
00:15:08,200 --> 00:15:14,750
you can just change things in one place again, which is in this case the model, and then regenerate the application.

129
00:15:14,750 --> 00:15:17,069
So, this also makes you more Agile.

130
00:15:17,079 --> 00:15:25,630
Another thing is if you have a good DSL that represents your domain concepts nicely, then it becomes feasible to do pair programming with domain experts.

131
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:31,069
I'm not saying that, you know, DSLs are so good that your customer can do the programming for you.

132
00:15:31,069 --> 00:15:35,350
That's probably an illusion. But you can actually uh sit together in front of a machine.

133
00:15:35,350 --> 00:15:38,230
You have your domain expert.

134
00:15:38,230 --> 00:15:41,430
You have your um programmer who uses your DSLs to build models.

135
00:15:41,440 --> 00:15:52,870
And then if the domain expert wants something different, has a different requirement, you change things, press a button, and a couple of minutes later, depending how long your generator runs, you have a running application.

136
00:15:52,870 --> 00:15:58,069
So, this this um customer integration can be can be improved significantly.

137
00:15:58,069 --> 00:16:04,030
Another thing is that if you talk to many of the Agile folks, then they're really big fans of automating all kinds of things.

138
00:16:04,030 --> 00:16:05,550
They want automated tests.

139
00:16:05,550 --> 00:16:07,670
They want automated build.

140
00:16:07,670 --> 00:16:11,949
They want automated creation of documentation, at least JavaDoc and these kinds of things.

141
00:16:11,949 --> 00:16:20,551
So, using automation to generate some of the code and to, you know, do some of the tedious work is just a logical consequence. It's just the next step.

142
00:16:20,560 --> 00:16:25,990
So, I don't think there is any difference in mindset here.

143
00:16:25,990 --> 00:16:30,270
So, I think MDSD and Agile development go together really well.

144
00:16:30,270 --> 00:16:32,150
Now, of course, there is Oh, yeah, testing.

145
00:16:32,150 --> 00:16:38,350
Um many people um equal Agile development with test driven.

146
00:16:38,350 --> 00:16:39,870
And of course, you can do the same in model driven development.

147
00:16:39,870 --> 00:16:46,510
You can first, for example, um come up with your component structures in a model.

148
00:16:46,510 --> 00:16:48,551
You generate the interfaces. Then you write a test case.

149
00:16:48,560 --> 00:16:52,910
And then you implement your business logic uh until the test shows green.

150
00:16:52,910 --> 00:16:56,829
So, you can do this approach in exactly the same way as as before.

151
00:16:56,829 --> 00:17:00,750
Now, there are two things that that is that is important to say that things work.

152
00:17:00,750 --> 00:17:07,829
One is domain architectures like those DSLs and metamodels, they're built on experience.

153
00:17:07,829 --> 00:17:14,870
We don't want to come up with a big design up front where there is a magic team of architects that sits into a room for half a year,

154
00:17:14,870 --> 00:17:23,429
comes up with the architecture and the generator and then people have to use it no matter whether it really solves anybody's problem.

155
00:17:23,429 --> 00:17:30,750
So, it's really really really really really important to build those meta artifacts, the domain architecture as you go in the project.

156
00:17:30,750 --> 00:17:42,390
And that also means that you have to have people on the project who are able and good enough and fast enough to respond to changes or to to implement changes in the domain architecture.

157
00:17:42,390 --> 00:17:53,070
So, if the application developers tell you the generator is missing a feature or something doesn't work and something is wrong and the architecture doesn't work, then you have to be able to change these things and not,

158
00:17:53,070 --> 00:17:57,310
you know, within 6 weeks because then your application developers get frustrated and you're screwed.

159
00:17:57,310 --> 00:18:05,350
So, what you have to do is to make sure you have a team of people who can respond to changes in requirements with regards to the domain architecture quickly.

160
00:18:05,350 --> 00:18:09,830
And that is that is the real challenge in model driven development,

161
00:18:09,830 --> 00:18:18,190
to coordinate the domain architecture development with application development because application development is driven by short-term goals and

162
00:18:18,190 --> 00:18:23,030
deadlines by the customer and you know, we need this feature tomorrow otherwise the customer's gone.

163
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:26,270
Whereas the domain architecture development has more this, you know, mid-term thing.

164
00:18:26,270 --> 00:18:28,190
You want to build an infrastructure you can reuse.

165
00:18:28,200 --> 00:18:29,790
You want to do the architecture right.

166
00:18:29,800 --> 00:18:34,790
You want to make sure things are clean and nice.

167
00:18:34,790 --> 00:18:41,830
So, aligning those two goals and also making compromises in both domains from time to time and then maybe fixing them later.

168
00:18:41,830 --> 00:18:49,950
Like you have to do some hack in the generator to make application developers happy, but then in the next iteration, you um you fix the generator and do it nicely.

169
00:18:49,960 --> 00:18:52,030
That's the real challenge.

170
00:18:52,030 --> 00:19:03,870
And doing that iteratively in in for example, you know, a new generator release every week and then also make sure that the generator knows about the different model versions and can handle these things.

171
00:19:03,870 --> 00:19:06,590
That is non-trivial and that is also where experience is required.

172
00:19:06,590 --> 00:19:15,870
Come you know, understanding those tools and building nice DSLs is not that complicated, but making that work in like projects with more than five people, that's certainly a challenge.

173
00:19:15,880 --> 00:19:17,870
It's comparable to building frameworks.

174
00:19:17,880 --> 00:19:24,110
And we all know that if you don't manage that carefully, you get really cool frameworks that nobody needs.

175
00:19:24,120 --> 00:19:36,230
Yeah, so I think um that was quite a lot of really interesting experiences and explanations about MDSD and models and model driven approaches and so on.

176
00:19:36,230 --> 00:19:45,030
And I think it's really a good idea to take a look at this yourself and try to figure out how it could work for you.

177
00:19:45,040 --> 00:19:57,190
And uh there are some um some links on our home on our homepage which is http um se-radio.net.

178
00:19:58,990 --> 00:20:01,031
So, one thing I want to add at at the at the end. So, two things.

179
00:20:01,031 --> 00:20:04,511
First of all, try it. It's First of all, it's fun.

180
00:20:04,520 --> 00:20:07,870
And second, it's much easier than you might think.

181
00:20:07,870 --> 00:20:16,030
And the second thing is that uh we we just scratched the surface in this episode, and we will probably have additional episodes on this topic down the road.

182
00:20:16,030 --> 00:20:20,070
Not the next subsequent ones, but from time to time we'll catch up this topic again and again.

183
00:20:20,070 --> 00:20:28,310
And uh one thing we might certainly will take a look at is, for example, doing, you know, discussing things a bit more practical.

184
00:20:28,310 --> 00:20:30,830
Like, you know, what tools do you use?

185
00:20:30,830 --> 00:20:32,430
